Geometry/ftt survey + alignment slots for the sTGC
==================================================

Three tables per slot: stgcOnTpc (1 row), stationOnStgc (4), pentOnStation (16).
Row order is what AGML's <Misalign row="N"/> indexes; pentOnStation row =
4*station + pent, pent order A,D,C,B (script/fttQuadConvention.C).

StFttDb composes them as

    AGML placeholder + stgcOnTpc + stationOnStgc[station] + pentOnStation[row]

with the placeholder = (phx[pent], 5.9, zstation) cm, phx = {0,0,-6.5,+6.5},
zstation = {312.342, 329.953, 347.637, 365.422}.  Translations are cm; StFttDb's
X/Y shifts are mm, so t0/t1 are scaled by 10 and t2 adds straight to z.

Slots, Run 22 (Gene's rule: one date per run year, just before the run; the
day-15..20 window is the safe one, and -10 ideal is outside it on purpose):

  -10  20211010.000000  ideal: all identity. The symmetric placeholder AGML
                        started from, before the detector was built.
  -11  20211011.000000  SURVEY: the numbers that were hardcoded in StFttDb as
                        X_shift_Quad*/Y_shift_Quad*/idealPlaneZLocations_Quad*.
                        Survey of the built detector, no alignment folded in.
  -15  20211015.000001  baseline: that survey PLUS the first FST->sTGC alignment
                        (the row comments carry the geo/align split).
  -16  --               the alignment program's lane, iterations in the seconds
                        field. Day -16 00:00:00 stays empty on purpose so an sdt
                        naming only that date falls back to the -15 baseline.
  -20  --               kept free for "ask with this one".

The -11 slot is a whole day wide (nothing is written at -12..-14), so a job can
name it as sdt20211012.000000 without hitting a sub-minute gap.

Why -11 exists at all: the hardcoded numbers are survey, not design, and until
now they lived only in the code -- and only there, since every -15 and later
entry has the survey ADDED to an alignment and cannot be separated from it by a
timestamp. Ideal (all zeros) is up to 48.49 mm and 2.91 mm in z away from them,
~15 strips at the 3.2 mm pitch, so "no DB" and "ideal" are not the same
geometry and the difference is not negligible.

Verify before pushing, end to end through StFttDb, nothing inlined:

    root4star -l -b -q 'script/fttSurveySlotCheck.C(20211012)'   # PASS, 48/48 exact
    root4star -l -b -q 'script/fttSurveySlotCheck.C(20220322)'   # real data, unchanged

DB side, 2026-11-06: UNBLOCKED. Dmitry created stgcOnTpc, stationOnStgc and
pentOnStation in Geometry_ftt on 2026-11-05, with StationIDs (4) and PentIDs
(16) for the two indexed ones. All 29 columns of stgcOnTpc are identical to the
proven Geometry_fst.fstOnTpc; the Nodes rows carry structName=Survey and
indexName Station/Pent, which is the elementID=None style Geometry_tpc and
Geometry_svt use, so the element list comes from the *IDs tables rather than a
1-N range as in Geometry_fst. akio has SELECT,INSERT on Geometry_ftt -- INSERT
only, so a wrong row cannot be deleted by us, only superseded by a later
beginTime or deactivated by the DBAs. One cosmetic difference: schema.type for
Id is "long" here and "int" in all seven other Geometry databases. Harmless --
the 4 padding bytes after "int Id" make the offsets and the 184-byte struct
size identical either way, and the stored row reads back Id=1.

The push macro is fttGeometry_db.C in the parent directory.

First slot PUSHED 2026-11-06, one row, as the round-trip test Dmitry asked for:

    root4star -l -b -q '.../fttGeometry_db.C("run22ideal","writedb","stgcOnTpc")'

Deliberately the identity slot, so the push is a no-op by construction: the
table was empty, and identity adds no misalignment. Verified four ways -- the
row on the master (all 20 Survey members exact, comment=0x49 'I', which is what
the STAR one-byte memcpy convention stores), 1 row on all eight read servers,
a read back through St_db_Maker/StDbBroker/StDbLib giving Id=1 and identity,
and the beginTime boundary (nothing at 2021-10-09, the row at 2021-10-11 and at
2025-03-15). The other two tables correctly answer "has No data for query".

This now matters to production in a way it did not before: PR #20 has landed,
so DEV's xgeometry.so (relinked 2026-11-06) carries 34 Geometry/ftt <Misalign>
paths and every AGML build queries these three tables. PR #23 has NOT landed --
DEV's StFttDbMaker has no loadGeometry at all -- so AGML is the only consumer
today. Pushing anything NON-identity changes production geometry.

Still to do: stationOnStgc and pentOnStation at run22ideal, then the remaining
ten tags. Push all three tables of a tag together -- StFttDb::loadGeometry()
chains them, so a partial tag leaves the geometry inconsistent at that stamp.
Reading a stored row back needs StUtilities and StDbBroker loaded before
St_db_Maker, or root4star dies on an undefined StMessageManager::Instance.
